welcome back so we're talking about  machine learning models from data and I  want to talk a little bit about where  neural networks and artificial  intelligence fit into this because  there's all of these different kind of  words and phrases for for machine  learning so again the neural networks  are super important we use them all the  time they're a lot of the technology  that is used in your daily life like  Alexa or Siri or Google Images and  Facebook they are built on kind of  neural network architectures for for  their their technology but I do want to  emphasize that neural networks are  really kind of a subset not an equal  subset but really a subset of machine  learning ok so they're an important part  of the larger family of machine learning  the other thing I want to talk about is  artificial intelligence so you'll hear  this all the time now artificial  intelligence AI and I think this is very  fuzzy kind of murky so machine learning  is pretty well defined nowadays neural  networks  you know we I'll give you a few lectures  in a series on what neural networks are  I can explain what they are to you  artificial intelligence is a little bit  more nebulous I mean generally speaking  it's this this idea that what we want is  a collection of algorithms and  experience and data an expert system  that can ask its own questions gather  its own data decide on the architecture  for learning really do some of the  things that biological systems can do so  you know a cat and a dog or  fundamentally curious and they go and  explore and they learn on their own and  they have a framework but it's kind of  an overarching framework where they can  you know learn how to learn better over  time and so that's very cool and there's  lots of interesting applications of  artificial intelligence and it hopes I  think to get towards some of these  aspects of more generalizability or you  know  if more higher hierarchies of models and  models of models and things like that  but I also want to point out that at  least in well I think in lots of fields  and in popular and technical fields  there's what I'll call a lot of  ballooning statements a lot of  ballooning statements about about AI so  people are using this word this buzzword  to be a catch-all for everything they're  gonna do to solve world hunger and you  know everything in the world is gonna be  solved because of artificial  intelligence and I think there's a lot  of ballooning statements these kind of  ballooned statements that people make  about how big artificial intelligence is  and how close we are and what the  capabilities are and I'll just give you  some examples you know or some some  anecdotes so most machine learning  algorithms that we're talking about most  of these these architectures where you  can build models from data they have  parameters hyper parameters that you  have to tune how fast you train them how  many degrees of freedom things like that  there's hyper parameters in a neural  network how many layers and how many  nodes and there is still kind of this  overarching process when you train these  models of turning those hyper parameters  until until they work better okay and  usually that is a human guided process  an expert human changes the architecture  and tweaks those hyper parameters and I  mean there are certainly efforts to  autumn to make that automatic and to  have kind of a meta learning theory  where you can have machines teaching  other machines based on what they've  learned so this is happening but I will  be honest in my estimation or at least  where we are right now there's still a  huge gap between kind of the human  guided expertise and  even remotely what we can do in  artificial intelligence okay so I don't  want to be too pessimistic pessimistic I  think there is a tremendous amount of  potential for expert systems for systems  that can learn to learn and that can  explain themselves and that can go be  curious and gather new data to 